diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the simple_bgp topology finds no network anomaly. The user's report of roughly one-in-three packet loss between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) is not supported by any measurable evidence in the live lab.

Specifically: a 60-packet rapid ping from pc1 to pc2 traversing both routers completed with 0% loss and stable RTT (0.5–1.5 ms). Every potential drop mechanism was checked and ruled out. The traffic-control subsystem on every interface (router1:eth0, router1:eth1, router2:eth0, router2:eth1, pc1:eth0, pc2:eth0) carries only the default fq_codel qdisc with zero dropped packets and no netem loss emulator. The iptables filter, mangle, and nat tables on all devices are completely empty. Interface-level RX/TX drop counters are all at zero. No cron jobs or background processes that could periodically inject drop rules were found.

The BGP control plane is equally healthy: the eBGP session between router1 (AS1) and router2 (AS2) has been stable since startup, both prefixes are correctly exchanged (195.11.14.0/24 learned by router2, 200.1.1.0/24 learned by router1), and the FRR log shows no route flapping. Startup scripts contain no fault injections.

The root cause of the ticket is a false premise: the path from pc1 to pc2 is fully operational and no device is dropping traffic. No corrective action is required; the user should retest and verify their measurement methodology.
